course: dictionary
lesson: 7109
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Peck is a word with several meanings. The clues in the sentence tell you which one you need. «Some woodpeckers peck holes in trees to make their nests.» Holes and trees tell you that peck means to hit with a beak.
explain: The same word can be a noun, a thing, or a verb, an action. «She gave her grandma a peck on the cheek.» Here a peck is a quick, light kiss. Peck can also name an old measure of grain, about 9 litres.
explain: Some meanings are only in special phrases. «A flock has a pecking order: the top hen eats first.» Pecking order is not about pecking at all. It means who is the boss in a group, and the phrase came from chickens.
fact: Peck can be a verb (to peck, pecks, pecked, pecking) or a noun (a peck, pecks).
fact: «Peck» means to hit or pick up something with the beak, as birds do.
fact: «A peck» can mean a quick, light kiss on the cheek.
fact: «A peck» is also an old measure for dry things like grain and fruit: about a quarter of a bushel, or about 9 litres.
fact: «Peck at your food» means to eat only a little, without being hungry.
exercise: «Some woodpeckers peck about 20 times a second.» What does peck mean here? | A quarter of a bushel | *To hit quickly with a beak | A quick, light kiss | To eat only a little
exercise: Mum says, «Give me a peck on the cheek.» What does peck mean? | A hard knock with a beak | A bird's call | *A quick, light kiss | A measure of grain
exercise: «A flock has a pecking order: the top hen eats first.» What does pecking order mean? | The order in which birds fly south | The way hens are fed on a farm | A list of the birds' names | *Who is the boss in a group
test: «She just pecked at her dinner and left most of it.» What does pecked at mean here? | Ate it very quickly | *Ate only a little, without much hunger | Threw it in the bin | Cooked it for a long time
test: «Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers.» In this old rhyme, what is a peck? | *An old measure of dry things, about 9 litres | A loud sound made by a bird | A kind of hat worn by farmers | A small bird with a sharp beak
exam: Which sentence uses peck as a verb meaning to hit with a beak? | «Peter Piper picked a peck of peppers.» | *«The woodpecker began to peck a hole in the tree.» | «Give your gran a peck on the cheek.» | «The farmer bought a peck of apples.»
